%% The Topic 4 revision sheet — included by KB Part Five and by the RN volume. Must make sense on its own.

## 1. Development and why gender matters

- **Development** — improvement in people's lives: income, health, education, freedom, choice; not only **GDP** (value of goods and services produced; ignores unpaid work).
- **History of the idea** — **Truman's "Point Four"** (**20 Jan 1949**, "underdeveloped areas") → 1950s–60s **growth and trickle-down** → 1970s **basic needs** and the discovery of women (**Boserup 1970**) → 1980s **structural adjustment** → **human development** (**Mahbub ul Haq**, UNDP HDR **1990**: "enlarging people's choices") → **Sen**, *Development as Freedom* (**1999**, capabilities = real freedoms) → **MDGs 2000** → **SDGs 2015** ("leave no one behind").
- **Why gender matters** — women are half the population; their **unpaid work** is uncounted (**Waring**, *If Women Counted*, 1988); policies are **never gender-neutral**; equality is a right (CEDAW, SDG 5) and also helps development (education, child health, household spending, growth); World Bank **WDR 2012** "**smart economics**" (critics **Chant and Sweetman 2012**: women as instruments, not rights-holders).
- **Measures** — **HDI** (1990; not a gender index); **GDI** and **GEM** (UNDP **1995**); **GII** (Gender Inequality Index, **2010**); WEF **Global Gender Gap Index** (2006).
- **Pakistan** — GGGR 2026 **143/145**, **59.5%**; economic **144th**, education 130th, political 107th (2025: 148/148); LFPR **24.4%** (LFS 2024–25); Ayub's "Decade of Development", Green Revolution; Haq's "**22 families**" (1968); first IMF SAP **1988**; **BISP 2008** (cash to women).
- **Four questions of gender analysis** — who works (paid/unpaid)? who owns and controls? who benefits? do gender relations become more equal?

## 2. Colonial perspectives of gender (2020, 2023, 2024)

- **Meaning** — how colonial rulers **saw, judged and changed** gender relations, and how the colonised responded: (1) the **colonial gaze**; (2) **colonial policies**; (3) the **nationalist response**.
- **The gaze** — **James Mill**, *History of British India* (**1817**): women's status = measure of civilisation, India judged low; **Katherine Mayo**, *Mother India* (**1927**): Indian men degenerate, women victims (Gandhi: "a drain inspector's report"); **Spivak** (1988): "White men are saving brown women from brown men"; **Lata Mani**: in the *sati* debate women were the "**ground**", not subjects; justified the "**civilising mission**".
- **Laws** — Bengal Sati Regulation **1829** (Roy); Hindu Widows' Remarriage Act **1856** (widows lost late husband's property); **Indian Penal Code 1860** (adultery s. 497 punished only the man, wife as husband's property); Cantonment and **Contagious Diseases Acts** 1864–68 (forced examination of women); Female Infanticide Prevention Act 1870; **Criminal Tribes Act 1871** (*hijras*); **Punjab Laws Act 1872** (custom first → Muslim women lost Islamic inheritance); Age of Consent Act 1891 (10 → 12); **Sarda Act 1929**; Shariat Act 1937; Dissolution of Muslim Marriages Act 1939.
- **Four channels** — **law** (codified personal law with male scholars and landlords → rigid, patriarchal "tradition"; "Anglo-Muhammadan law"); **land** (settlements recorded land in male heads' names; women's use rights lost); **economy** (machine cloth destroyed women's spinning and weaving; cash crops, plantations, cantonments); **education** (some girls' schools; a small educated elite of women).
- **Nationalist response** — **Partha Chatterjee** (1989): outer **material** world (copy the West) vs inner **spiritual** world of home (keep "Indian"); women as guardians of the inner world; the "new woman" — educated but modest; Muslim reformers (**Nazir Ahmad** 1869; **Mumtaz Ali** 1898): education within Islamic family values → women as symbols of honour and identity.
- **Assessment** — gains (sati ban, remarriage, age of consent, girls' schools, 1939 divorce law) vs losses (patriarchal codification, lost inheritance and land, criminalised *hijras*, bodily control, women used to justify rule); reforms often from Indian reformers. **Verdict:** colonialism **rearranged patriarchy**.
- **Legacy in Pakistan** — PPC 1860; Sarda Act until Sindh 2013 / ICT 2025; low land ownership and customary denial of inheritance; stigma of *khawaja siras*; women as symbols of identity.

## 3. Capitalist perspectives and "development as coloniality" (2022, 2023, 2024)

- **Capitalism** — private ownership, markets, wage labour, profit. Two perspectives: **liberal/market** (jobs and income free women; remove barriers; smart economics) vs **critical/socialist-feminist** (capitalism depends on and deepens subordination).
- **Critical ideas** — **Engels 1884** (private property → control of women's sexuality → "world historical defeat of the female sex"); industrial capitalism's **public/private split**, **separate spheres**, male **breadwinner**; **unpaid labour/social reproduction** (Benston 1969; Dalla Costa and James 1972); women as **cheap, flexible labour / reserve army**; **Maria Mies** (1986) — "**housewifisation**" (defining women as housewives to cheapen their work; lace makers of **Narsapur**); **Federici**, *Caliban and the Witch* (2004) — witch-hunts and early capitalism.
- **Pakistan** — home-based workers (**Sindh HBW Act 2018**); textiles and the **Ali Enterprises** fire (Karachi, **11 Sept 2012**, ~260 dead); cotton pickers; unpaid care; LFPR 24.4%.
- **Colonial + capitalist linked** — colonialism was capitalist expansion (East India Company); both created the public/private split; both extracted value from women's cheap or unpaid work; both used "civilisation/progress" to justify power.
- **Coloniality** — **Aníbal Quijano** (2000): **colonialism** (formal rule, ended) vs **coloniality** (continuing patterns of power in race, economy, knowledge); **María Lugones** (2007): **coloniality of gender** — European binaries and male-headed households imposed.
- **Post-development** — **Escobar**, *Encountering Development* (1995); **Sachs**, *The Development Dictionary* (1992): development as a discourse that defines the South as "underdeveloped" and makes Western experts the authorities.
- **Thesis applied to gender** — the "Third World woman" as victim (**Mohanty 1984**); outside experts set agendas; debt and conditions (SAPs, IMF); extraction via supply chains; Western gender models imposed.
- **Counter-argument** — vaccines, schools, lower maternal and child deaths; Southern ownership (Haq; **DAWN 1984**); women themselves demand education and rights. **Line:** development must be **decolonised, not abandoned** — owned by local women, focused on power.

## 4. Modernisation theory (2019, 2021, 2024)

- **Definition** — poor countries are poor because "**traditional**"; they develop by following the West: industry, cities, education, democracy, modern values; obstacles are **internal** (culture, religion, family).
- **Rostow**, *The Stages of Economic Growth: A Non-Communist Manifesto* (**1960**) — **traditional society → preconditions for take-off → take-off → drive to maturity → high mass consumption**; aid and investment help "take-off"; a Cold War theory. Also **Parsons** (pattern variables), **Lerner** (*Passing of Traditional Society*, 1958), **Inkeles and Smith** (*Becoming Modern*, 1974).
- **On women** — tradition oppresses, modernity frees; benefits **trickle down** through the male breadwinner; women as **mothers and homemakers** (welfare, nutrition, family planning); Western nuclear family as model.
- **2021 statement** — correct as description: modernisation located causes **inside** societies (culture, purdah, honour, parents' attitudes, traditional values); cure = education, media, urbanisation, Western values.
- **Gender critique** — **Boserup** (1970): modernisation gave land, technology, training and credit to men → women farmers marginalised; **female (hoe) vs male (plough) farming systems**; blames culture and ignores **structures** (colonial history, trade, land, state); West has its own patriarchy (new forms: housewife ideal, beauty industry, double burden); trickle-down failed within households; "tradition" often colonial or changing; ignores women's **agency**.
- **Still relevant** — education, cities, media and paid work do widen choices; some cultural practices are real obstacles (child marriage, honour killing, inheritance denial). Weakness: culture as the **only** cause, West as the **only** model.
- **Pakistan** — Ayub era (1958–69), Green Revolution (machines, credit to male landowners; poor women lost farm work), **MFLO 1961** as a modernising reform; today girls' education and phones widen options but LFPR stays 24.4% → structures matter.

## 5. Dependency theory

- **Definition** — poverty is **produced** by colonialism and **unequal exchange**; the rich **centre** extracts from the **periphery**; underdevelopment is a **result**, not a stage.
- **Thinkers** — **Prebisch** (ECLA, 1950; Prebisch–Singer thesis: falling terms of trade for raw materials); **Andre Gunder Frank** ("development of underdevelopment", **1966**); **Walter Rodney** (*How Europe Underdeveloped Africa*, 1972); **Samir Amin** (delinking); **Cardoso and Faletto** (1969, "dependent development").
- **Policy** — import-substituting industrialisation; protection; planning; South–South cooperation; **New International Economic Order** (UN, **1974**).
- **Offers gender analysis** — structures not culture; colonial history; explains cheap (increasingly female) labour; links gender to class.
- **Gender critique** — **gender-blind** (nations and classes, not women and men); women's **unpaid subsistence work subsidises** cheap exports (Mies); ignores **local patriarchy**; male nationalist industrial models; "Asian tigers" grew by export-led growth relying on young women's labour.
- **Feminist uses** — "double dependency" (country on centre; women on men); **Helen Safa** on Latin American women workers.
- **Pakistan** — cotton and textiles; cotton pickers; IMF debt limits social spending; but feudal land, custom and mobility limits also matter.

## 6. World-system theory and "gendered globalisation" (2025)

- **Wallerstein**, *The Modern World-System* (**1974**; MCQ 2019) — one capitalist **world-economy** since the 1500s; **core** (rich, finance, technology), **semi-periphery** (both exploiter and exploited), **periphery** (raw materials, cheap labour); surplus flows via **unequal exchange**; differs from dependency by one system, the semi-periphery and long history.
- **Gender extensions** — **Smith and Wallerstein** (1992): households combine wages with unpaid work, so employers pay **below a living wage** — women's unpaid labour fills the gap; **Mies** (1986): exploitation of nature, colonies and women; **global assembly line** (**Fuentes and Ehrenreich 1983**); **Kathryn Ward** (1990).
- **Gendered globalisation** — globalisation relies on, reshapes and often deepens gender inequality, while creating some opportunities.
- **New forms of patriarchal power** — **corporate/workplace patriarchy** (low wages, surveillance, harassment); **feminisation of labour** (**Standing 1989**: flexible, informal, low-paid); **global care chains** (**Hochschild 2000**); **debt and austerity**; **commodification of bodies** (trafficking, surrogacy, beauty industry); **cultural backlash**.
- **Evidence** — harm: **Rana Plaza** (Bangladesh, **24 April 2013**, 1,100+ dead, mostly women); **Ali Enterprises** (2012). Gains: first independent income for millions of women; later marriage; bargaining power (**Kabeer**, *The Power to Choose*, 2000).
- **Evaluation** — globalisation creates opportunities **and** new patriarchal controls; effect depends on labour rights, state policy, women's organising; theory risks portraying women as passive and underplays local patriarchy and agency.
- **Pakistan** — periphery/lower semi-periphery (textiles, cotton, rice; external finance); women at bottom of chains; male Gulf migration; remittances record **38+ billion dollars** (2024–25); EU **GSP+** (2014; 27 conventions incl. CEDAW).

## 7. Structural functionalism (2019 note)

- **Definition** — society as a **system** of interrelated parts (family, education, religion, economy, government), each with a **function** keeping the whole **stable** (equilibrium). MCQ 2019: related to **stability**.
- **Thinkers** — **Durkheim** (shared values, division of labour); **Talcott Parsons**; **Robert Merton** (manifest, latent functions; dysfunctions); **George Murdock** (**1949**: nuclear family universal; **sexual, reproductive, economic, educational** functions).
- **Parsons and Bales** (**1955**) — husband-father **instrumental** role (breadwinner, link to society, task-oriented); wife-mother **expressive** role (care, emotion, socialising children); family functions: primary socialisation and stabilising adult personalities; avoids competition between spouses.
- **Link to development** — pattern variables → modernisation; planners treated men as producers, women as homemakers (home-economics classes; extension services to men).
- **Critique** — **justifies inequality** ("functional for whom?"); ignores **power and conflict** (violence, control of money); based on **1950s white middle-class America**; ignores women's economic work; cannot explain **change**; biological assumptions. Critics: **Friedan** (1963), **Oakley** (*Sociology of Housework*, 1974), Marxist feminists.
- **Useful** — roles tied to institutions; **socialisation**; **dysfunction** (rigid roles waste talent).
- **Pakistan** — man as provider (*kafeel*), woman as homemaker in textbooks, sermons, dramas; but rural women do heavy productive work; two incomes needed; migration leaves women heads of households.

## 8. The theories compared (2024)

- **Critical gender analysis** asks: cause of poverty; where women appear; assumptions (family, work); silences; policy effects.
- **Table in words** — modernisation: internal tradition; women freed by modernity; critique = blames culture, men captured benefits. Dependency: external exploitation; women absent; critique = gender-blind, ignores local patriarchy and unpaid work. World-system: one unequal world economy; later households and assembly line; critique = early gender blindness, women as victims. Functionalism: incomplete modernisation; women expressive; critique = justifies inequality, ignores power.
- **Shared weaknesses** — gender blindness; **unpaid work invisible**; household as harmonious unit (vs **Sen's "cooperative conflict"**, 1990; **Agarwal's bargaining**, 1997); women passive; male-centred progress.
- **Responses** — WID (answer to modernisation); WAD (from dependency/Marxism); GAD (socialist feminism); later: capabilities (**Nussbaum 2000**), post-development and decolonial, feminist economics (Waring, Elson, Folbre), ecofeminism.
- **Conclusion** — each theory holds part of the truth (culture, institutions, global structures); gender analysis adds **power relations, unpaid work and agency**, joining local and global.

## 9. WID — Women in Development (1970s)

- **Origins** — before 1970, the **welfare approach** (women as mothers). **Boserup 1970**. Term coined by women development professionals in **Washington, DC** (MCQ 2018). **Percy Amendment 1973** (US aid must integrate women); **USAID WID Office 1974**; IWY 1975 and the UN Decade; women's ministries (**Pakistan's Women's Division 1979**).
- **Principles** — women **left out**; problem = exclusion, not the model; solution = **access** (education, credit, training, technology, jobs); **liberal feminism**; separate women's projects (MCQ 2019: women have an important role in development = WID).
- **Moser's approaches of the era** — **equity** (original WID; seen as threatening), **anti-poverty** (income projects), **efficiency** (women make development efficient; relies on unpaid time).
- **Practice** — sewing, handicrafts, poultry, kitchen gardens; credit; literacy and skills; appropriate technology (stoves, mills, pumps); women's units — critics: a "**women's ghetto**".
- **Achievements** — women visible in data and planning; institutions (women's ministries, the UN Decade fund later UNIFEM, INSTRAW); resources; women as producers; paved the way for CEDAW (1979).
- **Limitations** — accepted the development model ("**add women and stir**"); **took the sexual division of labour for granted** (MCQ 2024) → added to women's workload; women alone, not gender relations or men's power; ignored class, race, global inequality; small marginal projects in "feminine" activities; Western bias; instrumentalism. **Main criticism: failed to transform gender relations** (MCQ 2026).
- **Pakistan** — Women's Division (1979), Ministry of Women Development, vocational centres, credit schemes, **First Women Bank (1989)**: skills and some income, but small scale; little change in land, inheritance, wages or decisions.

## 10. WAD — Women and Development (late 1970s)

- **Origins** — Marxist and dependency critics of WID; IDS Sussex workshop "**The Continuing Subordination of Women in the Development Process**" (**1978**); Southern scholars.
- **Principles** — women **always part of development**, but on **unequal, exploitative terms**; the problem is the **nature of development** — unequal international and class system; women's paid and unpaid work undervalued; strategy: women's projects plus **structural change**.
- **Examples** — EPZ jobs = integration as exploitation; cash crops (who profits? world prices? food security?); aid projects that leave debt and trade untouched. "WID says let women in; WAD says women are already in — at the bottom."
- **NIEO link** (1974) — fairer world economy would help Southern women; shared North–South framing later criticised for hiding conflicts between Southern women and men.
- **Contributions** — "development for whom?"; links to class and global inequality; values women's knowledge; Southern voice; prepared GAD and DAWN.
- **Limitations** — **class over gender**; ignored patriarchy within classes; women as homogeneous; little on household and reproductive work; women-only projects again; weak policy influence.
- **Today** — the lens for judging micro-credit, handicraft projects, export jobs and donor NGO projects (2026 NGO-isation question).
- **Pakistan** — the Hyderabad embroiderer trained by a project still sells to a middleman at piece rates, owns no land, does all housework → position unchanged; **Okara** (2000–) and **Sindhiani Tehreek** (1982–) closer to WAD's idea of change.

## 11. GAD, needs, roles, empowerment, mainstreaming

- **Origins** — 1980s socialist feminists (IDS Subordination of Women group, **Kate Young**); **DAWN** (Bangalore **1984**; *Development, Crises and Alternative Visions* by **Gita Sen and Caren Grown**, Nairobi 1985); Beijing (1995) adopted mainstreaming and empowerment.
- **Principles** — **gender relations of power**, not women alone; gender socially constructed; production **and** reproduction, inside the household; women as **agents**; **transformation** of structures; role of the state; intersection with class and race. MCQ 2025: GAD focuses on "all of these".
- **Molyneux** (**1985**, Nicaragua) — practical vs strategic gender **interests**; **Moser** (1989, 1993) — practical and strategic gender **needs**.
- **PGNs** — immediate material needs from current roles (water, health, food, housing, fuel, income, childcare); accept division of labour; short term; welfare/anti-poverty/efficiency. MCQ 2026: they **address immediate necessities**.
- **SGIs/SGNs** — long-term interests in changing women's position (land and inheritance, laws against violence, equal wages, political power, bodily control); challenge division of labour; equity/empowerment. Example: hand pump near home (PGN) vs women's land ownership under the **2020** property law (SGN). Meeting PGNs through women's organisations can open strategic change.
- **Moser's triple role** — **productive**, **reproductive**, **community-managing** (men: productive and community-**politics**, paid, status). **Five approaches** — **welfare** (1950s, mothers), **equity** (1970s WID), **anti-poverty**, **efficiency** (1980s), **empowerment** (bottom-up, from the South).
- **Kabeer** — *Reversed Realities* (**1994**; MCQ 2018); empowerment (**1999**) = "expansion in people's ability to make **strategic life choices** where this ability was previously denied"; **resources, agency, achievements**. MCQ 2023: beneficiaries → active participants = empowerment. **Longwe**: welfare, access, conscientisation, participation, control. **Harvard Framework** (1985): activities, access and control.
- **Gender mainstreaming** — ECOSOC (**1997**): assessing the implications for women and men of any planned action, in all areas and at all levels; first adopted globally in the **Beijing** Declaration (**1995**; MCQ 2024); benefits "all of these" (MCQ 2025); tools: sex-disaggregated data, gender analysis, **gender budgeting**, audits, focal points, quotas.
- **Pakistan GAD tools** — Gender Reform Action Plans (2000s); gender-responsive budgeting pilots; **10% quota** in federal jobs; **BISP** cash to women; Women's Property Rights Act 2020; laws on violence and child marriage.
- **GAD limitations** — hard to implement (reduced to checklists and training); "gender" can hide women; "mainstreamed away"; depoliticisation (NGO-isation); resistance from men and elites; "Western concepts?" (reply: DAWN is Southern).
- **Comparison** — focus: women / women and class / gender relations. Problem: excluded / unequally integrated / unequal power. Theory: liberal & modernisation / Marxism & dependency / socialist feminism. Goal: integrate / change system / transform relations. Household: not examined / little / inside, bargaining. Needs: practical / practical / practical and strategic. Women: passive recipients / exploited workers / agents. Critique: status quo / ignores patriarchy / hard to implement. Names: Boserup, Percy / IDS 1978 / Molyneux, Moser, Kabeer, DAWN.

## 12. Structural adjustment (2022)

- **Background** — debt crisis (**Mexico 1982**); IMF/World Bank loans with conditions; "**Washington Consensus**" (**Williamson 1989**).
- **Conditions** — cut spending (health, education, subsidies, public jobs); remove subsidies (food, fuel, power); **user fees**; devaluation; privatisation; trade and financial liberalisation; labour deregulation.
- **Gender-blind** — ignored the **household/care economy**; costs shifted to women's unpaid work. **Diane Elson**, *Male Bias in the Development Process* (**1991**): SAPs treat women's labour as "**infinitely elastic**".
- **Critique** — more unpaid care (sick at home, stretching food); worse women's health (fees; maternal care); **girls withdrawn** from school; loss of public-sector jobs (teachers, nurses); push into informal work; export jobs low-paid and insecure; food price rises; stress and violence; men lose breadwinner role; **no voice** for women.
- **Evidence and response** — **UNICEF**, *Adjustment with a Human Face* (**Cornia, Jolly, Stewart 1987**); Commonwealth, *Engendering Adjustment for the 1990s* (**1989**); DAWN 1985; response: safety nets, **PRSPs (1999)**, "gender-responsive" language — core conditions little changed.
- **Pakistan** — first IMF SAP **1988**; **SBA 2023**; **7-billion-dollar EFF 2024**; conditions: energy prices, sales tax, development cuts, subsidy removal → women's unpaid work and budgets squeezed; **BISP expanded** as safety net (partial answer). **Line:** stabilisation may be needed but must be **gender-assessed** — protect health, education, BISP; progressive taxes; count care.

## 13. Globalisation (2017, 2018, 2019)

- **Definition** — growing **interdependence** of societies worldwide (MCQ 2023); **Giddens**: "intensification of worldwide social relations which link distant localities". Dimensions: economic, political, technological, cultural, human (migration). Accelerated from the 1980s; end of Cold War 1991; **WTO 1995**; internet.
- **Highlighting gender issues (2017)** — global norms and conferences (Beijing, CEDAW, SDG 5); media and #MeToo; supply-chain disasters (Rana Plaza); migration makes care visible.
- **Opportunities** — export jobs, first independent income (Bangladesh garments; East Asian electronics; Indian call centres; **Kabeer 2000**); information and mobile banking; rights norms (CEDAW, SDGs, **GSP+**); microfinance (**Grameen 1983**) and remittances; wider images of women.
- **Inequalities (2019)** — feminisation of labour (**Standing 1989**); EPZs (no unions, long hours, harassment); informal home-based subcontracting; wage gaps; **global care chains** (**Hochschild 2000**; *kafala* abuse); trafficking; agricultural displacement; unsafe workplaces (Rana Plaza 2013; Ali Enterprises 2012); commodification (skin-lightening creams) and backlash; inequality between countries.
- **Pakistan (2018)** — textiles and garments (home-based stitchers on piece rates); Sialkot's **Atlanta Agreement 1997** (stitching moved to centres; women homeworkers lost work); cotton pickers and world prices; male migration and remittances (38+ billion dollars, 2024–25; women run households); IMF programmes; **GSP+ 2014** (27 conventions incl. CEDAW); digital freelancing but 25% mobile gap; media and backlash; LFPR 24.4%, economic rank 144th — fewer women drawn into export jobs than in Bangladesh (mobility, transport, safety, industry structure).
- **Balance** — both good and bad; outcome depends on labour rights, social protection, transport and childcare, women's organisation, fair trade.

## 14. Climate change (2026)

- **Why gendered** — dependence on water, fuel, fodder; less land, savings, insurance; restricted mobility and warnings; care burdens; pregnancy and heat risks; little voice.
- **Ecofeminism** — domination of women and nature linked; coined by **d'Eaubonne** (**1974**); **Shiva**, *Staying Alive* (**1988**, "maldevelopment"); **Mies and Shiva**, *Ecofeminism* (1993); **Chipko** (1973); **Maathai**, Green Belt (1977; Nobel 2004). Risk: **essentialism**.
- **Feminist environmentalism** — **Bina Agarwal**, "The Gender and Environment Debate" (**1992**): women's relation to nature is **material** — work, property, power; explains variation among women; points to land rights and resources.
- **2022 floods** — ~**33 million** affected, 1,700+ dead, a third of the country under water; damage and losses ~**30 billion dollars**; ~**650,000 pregnant women** (UNFPA). Women's impacts: maternal care lost; camp privacy and safety (toilets, lighting, harassment); menstrual hygiene; lost livestock, gardens, home-based work; relief registered to **male heads** or needing **CNICs**; care for the sick; girls out of school; early marriage reports; mobility and warnings.
- **Heat waves** — **Jacobabad** among hottest cities; cooking over open fires, field work, pregnancy risks, longer water collection, dress and mobility limits, lost home-based income.
- **Critical analysis** — ecofeminism: common logic of domination, moral force; but essentialist and romanticising. Feminist environmentalism: explains **which** women suffer (no land, savings, CNIC, mobility, voice) and policy. Intersectionality: poor, rural, landless, minority, disabled women suffered most. **Use both.**
- **Frameworks** — Beijing area **K**; UNFCCC **Lima Work Programme on Gender (2014)**, **Gender Action Plan (2017)**; **Sendai Framework (2015)**; Pakistan **National Climate Change Policy (2012, updated 2021)**, **Climate Change Gender Action Plan (2022)**, **NDMA Gender and Child Cell**, BISP flood cash; Pakistan emits **< 1%** of global emissions; **loss and damage** fund (**COP27, 2022**).
- **Policy** — sex-disaggregated data; women in disaster management; women-friendly relief (toilets, lighting, female staff, sanitary kits, maternal care); cash to women and CNIC help; land rights; early warnings reaching women (phones, LHWs); climate-resilient livelihoods; protect girls' schooling; prevent early marriage.

## 15. Global goals and debates

- **MDGs (2000–15)** — 8 goals; **MDG 3** gender equality (target: education parity); **MDG 5** maternal health. Gender critique: narrow; left out violence, unpaid care, property. MCQ 2025: "equal political participation of women" was **not** an MDG (seats were only an indicator). Pakistan missed most targets.
- **SDGs (2015–30)** — **17** goals, **169** targets, universal, "leave no one behind"; **SDG 5**: "Achieve gender equality and empower all women and girls" (MCQ 2026). Targets: **5.1** discrimination; **5.2** violence; **5.3** child/forced marriage, FGM; **5.4** unpaid care; **5.5** leadership; **5.6** reproductive health; **5.a** economic resources, land; **5.b** technology; **5.c** laws and policies. Gender also in SDGs 1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 13, 16.
- **Pakistan** — NA adopted SDGs as national agenda (**Feb 2016**); Kathmandu 2017 South Asian meeting (MCQ 2018); slow SDG 5 progress (143/145; 24.4%; MMR 186, 2019; child marriage); positives: laws 2020–25, BISP, girls' enrolment, mobile use.
- **Assessment** — targets, data and attention vs voluntary, data gaps, financing, COVID-19 setbacks; goals help when domestic movements use them.
- **Debates** — **smart economics** (opens doors vs instrumentalism); **care economy** and the "**3 Rs**" (recognise, reduce, redistribute; SDG 5.4; day-care, leave, water, clean fuel); **IMF austerity** (gender-assess; protect BISP); **microfinance** (empowerment vs debt trap; context matters — Kabeer); **platform work and AI** (flexible income vs insecurity and automation); **climate justice** (will finance reach women?); **development as coloniality** (decolonise, don't abandon); **raising women's LFPR** (safe transport, childcare, harassment law, protected home-based and digital work).
